---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="How to Choose a Pickleball Paddle Shape | PaddleFitHub"
  description="Compare standard, hybrid, and elongated pickleball paddle shapes. Learn which shape fits your play style, court position, and priorities."
  canonicalPath="/blog/choosing-paddle-shape/"
>
  <div class="container-narrow py-16">
    <p class="text-caption-mono text-mute uppercase tracking-wide mb-2">Resource</p>
    <h1 class="text-display-xl text-ink mb-4">Choosing a Paddle Shape</h1>
    <p class="text-body-lg text-body mb-8">Standard, hybrid, or elongated? Each paddle shape trades off reach, sweet spot size, and maneuverability.</p>

    <div class="prose text-body-md text-body space-y-6 max-w-none">
      <h2 class="text-display-sm text-ink">Standard (~16" × 8")</h2>
      <p>The widest face with the largest sweet spot. Best for net play, dinking, and players who value forgiveness over reach. The easiest shape for beginners.</p>

      <h2 class="text-display-sm text-ink">Hybrid (~16.5" × 7.5")</h2>
      <p>The most versatile shape, adding about half an inch of reach without sacrificing too much sweet spot. A popular choice for all-around players.</p>

      <h2 class="text-display-sm text-ink">Elongated (~17" × 7")</h2>
      <p>Maximum reach and leverage for power. Ideal for baseline play and singles. The narrow face means a smaller sweet spot and steeper learning curve.</p>

      <h2 class="text-display-sm text-ink">Which should you choose?</h2>
      <p>There is no single right answer. Your game format (singles vs doubles), court position, and swing style all matter. Use the <a href="/tools/paddle-shape-fit/">Paddle Shape Fit</a> tool for a data-driven recommendation.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-hairline">
      <a href="/tools/paddle-shape-fit/" class="btn-primary">Try the Paddle Shape Fit Tool</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>